## Installation

The verified environment used Python 3.14.6 on Windows 11.

Create and activate a virtual environment:

```powershell
python -m venv .qiskit
.qiskit\Scripts\Activate.ps1
```

Install the project and development dependencies:

```powershell
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
python -m pip install -e ".[dev]"
```

Alternatively, recreate the recorded environment:

```powershell
python -m pip install -r requirements-lock.txt
```

## Running the tests

Run the complete test suite:

```powershell
python -m pytest -v
```

Current verified status:

```text
49 passed
```

The tests cover:

- circuit and probability validation;
- symmetric and asymmetric noise models;
- repetition- and surface-code circuit generation;
- detector error-model construction;
- decoder construction;
- logical-failure calculation;
- reproducible detector sampling;
- statistical intervals and rare-event bounds;
- batch and individual decoder benchmarking;
- queue utilisation, backlog and deadline behaviour.

## Reproducing the threshold study

The threshold collection uses Sinter with PyMatching and adaptive stopping conditions.

Run the collection script:

```powershell
python src\surface_code_threshold.py
```

Then run the analysis notebook:

```text
notebooks\surface_code_threshold_analysis.ipynb
```

The saved summary is:

```text
results\surface_code_threshold_summary.csv
```

The headline figure can be regenerated without repeating the Monte Carlo collection:

```powershell
python scripts\regenerate_threshold_figure.py
```

