## Limitations

- The threshold is a preliminary finite-distance crossing estimate rather than a full finite-size-scaling result.
- The noise models are stochastic Pauli models and do not include leakage, coherent calibration errors, crosstalk or temporally correlated faults.
- Minimum-weight perfect matching relies on a graph-like decomposition of the detector error model.
- CPU timing results contain Python-call, operating-system and measurement overhead.
- Batch throughput does not directly represent real-time decoder latency.
- The queue model resamples measured service times and does not yet include communication or control-system delays.
- The current analysis covers repetition and rotated surface codes rather than quantum LDPC codes.
- FPGA acceleration has not yet been implemented or synthesised.

## Future work

Potential extensions include:

1. Refine the surface-code threshold using distances 5, 7, 9 and 11 with denser sampling near the crossing region.
2. Compare logical \(X\)- and logical \(Z\)-memory thresholds.
3. Introduce correlated, biased and hardware-informed noise models.
4. Implement low-weight fault enumeration or importance sampling for rare logical failures.
5. Compare minimum-weight perfect matching with belief-propagation or hybrid decoders.
6. Incorporate decoder latency, communication bandwidth and power into quantum architecture resource estimates.
7. Develop and synthesise an FPGA-oriented message-passing decoder.
8. Extend the workflow to quantum LDPC codes and application-specific fault-tolerant architectures.

## Project status

The core simulation, decoding, statistical analysis and classical performance study are complete. The current focus is documentation, portability and preparation of a concise technical report.
